===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[weight]] of twenty [[grain]]s or one third of a [[dram]] (symbol: [[℈]]).
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} ''Hence'', a very small quantity; a [[particle]].
#* {{quote-book|author=William Shakespeare|authorlink=William Shakespeare|chapter=[[w:Twelfth Night|Tvvelfe Night, or What You Will]]|title=[[w:First Folio|Mr. William Shakespeares Comedies, Histories, & Tragedies: Published According to the True Originall Copies]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by [[w:William Jaggard|Isaac Iaggard]], and [[w:Edward Blount|Ed[ward] Blount]]|year=c. 1600–1601|year_published=1623|section=Act III, scene iv|page=267, column 2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uNtBAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA267|oclc=606515358|passage=Why euery thing adheres togither, that no dramme of a '''ſcruple''', no '''ſcruple''' of a '''ſcruple''', no obſtacle, no incredulous or unſafe circumſtance: What can be ſaide? Nothing that can be, can come betweene me, and the full proſpect of my hopes.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[doubt#Noun|doubt]] or [[uncertainty]] concerning a matter of fact; [[intellectual]] [[perplexity]].
# [[hesitation|Hesitation]] to act from the difficulty of determining what is [[right#Adjective|right]] or [[expedient]]; [[doubt#Noun|doubt]], [[hesitation]] or [[unwillingness]] due to [[motive#Noun|motives]] of [[conscience]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Thomas Babington Macaulay|authorlink=Thomas Babington Macaulay|chapter=John Bunyan|title=Biographical Essays. [...] Frederic the Great.—Bunyan.—Goldsmith.—Johnson.—Barère|series=Collection of British Authors|seriesvolume=CCCCV|edition=Tauchniz|location=Leipzig|publisher=[[w:Tauchnitz publishers|Bernhard Tauchniz]]|year=1857|page=99|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=jabYNNVDK7YC&pg=PA99|oclc=909866528|passage=The four chief sins of which he was guilty were dancing, ringing the bells of the parish church, playing at tipcat, and reading the History of Sir [[w:Bevis of Hampton|Bevis of Southampton]]. A Rector of the school of [[w:Wiliam Laud|[William] Laud]] would have held such a young man up to the whole parish as a model. But [[w:John Bunyan|[John] Bunyan]]'s notions of good and evil had been learned in a very different school; and he was made miserable by the conflict between his tastes and his '''scruples'''.}}
# A [[Hebrew]] [[unit]] of [[time#Noun|time]] [[equal]] to {{frac|1080}} [[hour]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Giles Strauchius [''i.e.'', {{w|Aegidius Strauch II}}]|author2={{w|Richard Sault}}, transl.|chapter=Of Minutes, Scruples and Moments|title=Breviarium Chronologicum. Or a Treatise Describing the Terms and Most Celebrated Characters, Periods and Epocha’s Used in Chronology. By Giles Strauchius, D.D. and Publick Professor in the University of Wittebergh. Now Done into English from the Third Edition, in Latin. By Richard Sault, F.R.S.|edition=2nd corrected|location=London|publisher=Printed for A. Bosvile at the Dial and Bible against [[w:St Dunstan-in-the-West|St. Dunstan's Church]] in [[w:Fleet Street|Fleet-street]]|year=1704|section=book I (Of some Terms in Chronology, and those the Most Common), §&nbsp;3|page=8|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=pSkIAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA8|oclc=912637911|passage=If it be ask'd why the Jews divide the Hour into 1080 '''Scruples''', the Author of the ''Neomeniæ'' ſet forth by ''Munſter'' gives this reaſon fo it, becauſe there is no number that is diviſible into ſo many ſorts as this of 1080 is; for it may be divided by 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 15, 20, &c.}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=George Greenwood|title=East of the Jordan|magazine=[[w:The Athenaeum (British magazine)|The Athenæum: Journal of English and Foreign Literature, Science, and the Fine Arts]]|location=London|publisher=Printed by James Holmes, Took's Court, {{w|Chancery Lane}}, published at the office, 20, Wellington Street, [[w:Strand, London|Strand]], W.C., by John Francis. [...]|date=23 July 1864|issue=1917|page=116|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=QpIeAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA116|column=1|oclc=956082422|passage=The most ancient hour was divided into 1,080 '''scruples'''. The Jews suppose {{w|Issachar}} to have brought it from heaven.}}

