===Verb===
{{en-verb|scrupl}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[hesitate]] or be [[reluctant]] to act due to [[consideration]]s of [[conscience]] or [[expedience]].
#* {{quote-book|author=Robert South|authorlink=Robert South|chapter=A True State and Account of the Plea of a Tender Conscience. In a Sermon Preach’d at [[w:Christ Church, Oxford|Christ-Church, Oxon.]] before the [[w:University of Oxford|University]], in Michaelmas Term, 1672|title=Twelve Sermons upon Several Subjects and Occasions|edition=5th|location=London|publisher=Printed by H. Clark, for Jonah Bowyer, at the Rose, the West-End of [[w:St Paul's Cathedral|St. Paul's Church-Yard]]|year=1672|year_published=1722|volume=III|page=206|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=YCUHAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA206|oclc=731567810|passage=But the ''Tenderneſs'', we have to deal with, is quite of another Nature, being ſuch as one as makes Men '''ſcruple''' at the Lawfulneſs of a Set Form of Divine Worship, at the Uſe of ſome Solemn Rites and Ceremonies in the Service of God; but makes them not ſtick at all at ''Sacrilege'', which St. ''Paul'' equates to ''Idolatry''; {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|author=[Thomas] Fuller|authorlink=Thomas Fuller|title=Good Thoughts in Bad Times|editor=W. T. Brantly|magazine=The Columbian Star and Christian Index|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Printed by Martin & Boden, no. 204 [[w:Market Street (Philadelphia)|Market Street]]|date=23 October 1830|volume=III|issue=17|page=263, column 2|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=4Vg4AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA263|oclc=699739666|passage=On the day wherein we receive the sacrament, we are often over-precise, '''scrupling''' to say or do those things which lawfully we may. But we, who are more than curious that day, are not so much as careful the next; and too often (what shall I say?) go on in sin up to the ancles; yea, our sins go over our heads.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[excite]] scruples in; to cause to scruple.
#* {{quote-book|author=Edw[ard] Symmons|chapter=Sect. IV. 1. The Nature of Their Charge Opened. 2. Their Vilanous and Bloudy Scope therein, Clearely Evidenced, and Proved. 3. How Perfectly in Their Tenents They Hold with the Jesuites in the Points of King-killing and King-deposing, Fully Declared.|title=A Vindication of King Charles: Or, a Loyal Subjects Duty. Manifested in Vindicating His Soveraigne from those Aspersions Cast upon Him by Certaine Persons, in a Scandalous Libel, Entituled, The Kings Cabinet Opened: And Published (as They Say) by Authority of Parliament. Whereunto is Added, a True Parallel betwixt the Sufferings of Our Saviour and Our Soveraign, in Divers Particulars, &c.|location=[London?|publisher=s.n.]|year=1648|page=37|pageurl=https://archive.org/stream/vindicationofkin00symm#page/37/mode/1up/|oclc=839300790|passage=It is granted indeed before that time, the Supream power was in Him &#91;{{w|Charles I of England}}&#93;, and we were all his Subjects: and then perhaps ſome might '''Scruple''' to cut his throat, for there were lawes then in force against Regicides, but now ſince his Reſignation, (for ſo in our ''Tenents'' we hold this Act to be,) there is no ſcruple to be made, thoſe lawes against King-killers are ſuſpended, and he is now become as ''Samſon'' was without his ſtrength, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To regard with [[suspicion]]; to [[question#Verb|question]].
#* {{quote-book|author=John Milton|authorlink=John Milton|title=[[w:Areopagitica|Areopagitica; a Speech of Mr. John Milton for the Liberty of Vnlicenc’d Printing, to the Parliament of England]]|location=London|publisher=[s.n.]|year=1644|page=7|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=tvhAAQAAMAAJ&pg=PA7|oclc=606676387|passage=As for the writings of Heathen authors, unleſſe they were plaine invectives againſt Chriſtianity, as thoſe of ''[[w:Porphyry (philosopher)|Porphyrius]]'' and ''{{w|Proclus}}'', they met with no interdict that can be cited, till about the year 400, in a ''Carthaginian'' Councel, wherein Biſhops themſelves were forbid to read the Books of Gentiles, but Hereſies they might read: while others long before them on the contrary '''ſcrupl'd''' more the Books of Hereticks, then of Gentiles.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|obsolete}} To question the [[truth]] of (a fact, etc.); to [[doubt#Verb|doubt]]; to hesitate to [[believe]], to question.
#: {{ux|en|I do not '''scruple''' to admit that all the Earth seeth but only half of the Moon.}}

